{"name":"Amélie’s Tutu","hex":"#fea7bd","fetched":"2026-10-21","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tutu","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A ballet skirt made of layered stiff but light netting."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of certain members of genus Coriaria of shrubs and trees found in New Zealand, including Coriaria arborea and Coriaria pottsiana, used by the Maori to make a sweetener, but toxic when not properly prepared."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tutu"}],"entries":[{"title":"Amélie","blocks":[{"text":"Amélie is a 2001 French-language romantic comedy film directed by Jean-Pierre Jeunet. Written by Jeunet with Guillaume Laurant, the film is a whimsical depiction of contemporary Parisian life, set in Montmartre. It tells the story of Amélie Poulain, played by Audrey Tautou, a shy and quirky waitress who decides to change the lives of those around her for the better while dealing with her own isolation. The film features an ensemble cast of supporting roles, including Mathieu Kassovitz, Rufus, Lorella Cravotta, Serge Merlin, Jamel Debbouze, Claire Maurier, Clotilde Mollet, Isabelle Nanty, Dominique Pinon, Artus de Penguern, Yolande Moreau, Urbain Cancelier, and Maurice Bénichou."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/53/Amelie_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Am%C3%A9lie","wikidata":"Q484048"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/53/Amelie_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/53/Amelie_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmelie_poster.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Paris_-_Caf%C3%A9_des_2_Moulins_-_2004.jpg/500px-Paris_-_Caf%C3%A